---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { streamsSidebar } from '../../../data/streams-sidebar';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Combining & Accumulating" sidebar={streamsSidebar} description="Combine multiple streams and accumulate values over time">

<h1>Combining &amp; Accumulating</h1>

<p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
  Merge streams together and build up state over time.
</p>

<h2>combinedWith</h2>

<p>Merge two streams with a combinator function. Publishes whenever either source changes:</p>

<Code code={`val a = StreamSource(1)
val b = StreamSource(10)

val sum = a.combinedWith(b) { x, y -> x + y }
println(sum())  // 11

a(5)
println(sum())  // 15

b(100)
println(sum())  // 105`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>pairedWith</h2>

<p>Shorthand for combining into a <code>Pair</code>:</p>

<Code code={`val pair = a.pairedWith(b)
println(pair())  // Pair(1, 10)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2 id="switchmap">switchMap</h2>

<p>Follow a stream that lives <em>inside</em> another stream's value. When the outer value changes, the old inner
  stream is unsubscribed and the new one is subscribed in its place:</p>

<Code code={`class Player(val diagnostics: Stream<Int>)

val playerA = Player(StreamSource(60))
val playerB = Player(StreamSource(30))

val players = StreamSource(playerA)
val fps = players.switchMap { it.diagnostics }

fps.subscribeToStream { println(it) }  // 60

players(playerB)                       // 30 — playerA's stream is released`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p>The selector runs on every value read while nothing is subscribed, and never at construction time — so keep
  it free of side effects.</p>

<p>Switching releases the old inner stream before subscribing the new one. An upstream shared by all the inner
  streams — one <code>ticker()</code> feeding every player — therefore sees its last subscriber leave and
  restarts on each switch.</p>

<Aside>We could have overlapped the two subscriptions instead, and then you would briefly own two live inner
  streams. Neither choice is free; this one at least never leaves you holding a stream you thought you had
  dropped. <code>permanent()</code> on the shared source is the escape hatch.
</Aside>

    <h3>switchMapNotNull</h3>

    <p>When the outer stream is nullable, <code>switchMapNotNull</code> runs the selector only for non-null values
      and publishes <code>null</code> the rest of the time:</p>

    <Code code={`val players = StreamSource<Player?>(null)

val fps: Stream<Int?> = players.switchMapNotNull { it.diagnostics }

println(fps())  // null — no player

players(playerA)
println(fps())  // 60

players(null)
println(fps())  // null again — the last value is NOT held`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

    <p>Unlike <code>foldNotNull</code> and <code>historyOfNonNull</code> further down this page, it resets rather
      than keeping what it had. Compose with <code>fallbackTo(0)</code> when you want a non-null result.</p>

    <h3>Returning the same stream twice</h3>

    <p>Inner streams are compared by identity. Selecting the same instance again keeps the subscription and
      publishes nothing — but a selector that <em>builds</em> a stream returns a new instance every time it runs,
      so it resubscribes on every outer value:</p>

    <Code code={`// Resubscribes on every outer value: map() allocates a new stream per call
players.switchMapNotNull { it.diagnostics.map { d -> d * 2 } }

// Stable instance: hoist the derived stream into the class
class Player(val diagnostics: Stream<Int>) {
    val doubled = diagnostics.map { d -> d * 2 }
}

players.switchMapNotNull { it.doubled }`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

    <p>Switching publishes the new inner stream's current value even when it equals the last one published. Add
      <code>.distinct()</code> if you only want changes.</p>

    <Aside>Switching releases the old inner stream <em>before</em> subscribing the new one. If every inner stream
      is derived from one shared upstream — say a single <code>ticker()</code> feeding all your players — that
      upstream sees its last subscriber leave and shuts down, then starts again a moment later. Usually invisible,
      unless the upstream is a coroutine that loses its rhythm. <code>permanent()</code> on the shared source
      keeps it alive.
    </Aside>

<h2>fold</h2>

<p>Accumulate values over time, like <code>List.fold()</code> but reactive:</p>

<Code code={`val clicks = StreamSource("click")
val count = clicks.fold(0) { acc, _ -> acc + 1 }

// count is 1 after initial subscribe, increments on each "click"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

    <h3>foldNotNull</h3>

    <p>Like <code>fold</code>, but skips null values in the source stream:</p>

    <Code code={`val source = StreamSource<String?>(null)
val lengths = source.foldNotNull(0) { acc, next -> acc + next.length }

println(lengths())  // 0

source("hello")
println(lengths())  // 5

source(null)        // skipped
println(lengths())  // 5

source("world")
println(lengths())  // 10`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

<h2>history</h2>

<p>Keep a sliding window of recent values:</p>

<Code code={`val source = StreamSource(1)
val recent = source.history(3)

source(2)
source(3)
source(4)

println(recent())  // [2, 3, 4] — oldest values dropped`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

    <h3>historyOfNonNull</h3>

    <p>Like <code>history</code>, but only keeps non-null values in the window:</p>

    <Code code={`val source = StreamSource<String?>("a")
val recent = source.historyOfNonNull(3)

source("b")
source(null)   // skipped
source("c")
source(null)   // skipped
source("d")

println(recent())  // ["b", "c", "d"] — nulls excluded`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

<div class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4">
  <a href="/ultra/streams/control" class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
